specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:171:- The check is load-bearing rather than decorative: reverting the T004 dependency move reproduces 8 `tsc` errors, and restoring the top-level Vertex import from before T002 reproduces `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` — both caught by this script alone.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:175:- The fixture calls `createHalEngine` and `start()` on port `0`, so the OS picks a free port and CI cannot collide with whatever else is listening.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:176:- `GET {basePath}/health` answers `200` with `status: ok` and a timestamp. This is what proves the runtime dependencies resolve from the tarball's own `dependencies` rather than from a lucky hoist.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:177:- One full turn runs through the orchestrator and the mock provider, and the assistant text is asserted. It is compared trimmed: the mock yields each word with a trailing space, so the last chunk leaves one behind — a fixture artefact rather than anything the orchestrator promises.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:178:- `stop()` is called, and the runner fails the variant if the process does not then exit on its own within 15 seconds. A smoke test that needs killing is hiding a leaked handle from every consumer, which the engine's unreferenced heartbeat timer once was. Proved by leaking a handle deliberately and watching the runner refuse it.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:179:- The consumer directory is asserted to be outside the repository rather than assumed to be: `mktemp` honours `TMPDIR`, and a `TMPDIR` inside the tree would quietly restore the resolution the whole script exists to prevent.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:183:- `bare` installs neither optional peer — a mock-provider consumer. It asserts both peers are absent, runs the engine, and type-checks.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:184:- `full` installs both at the versions `peerDependencies` names, and asserts both providers construct with their SDK loaded. Nothing here holds cloud credentials, so construction is the whole assertion; a call would fail for reasons that say nothing about this package.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:185:- Neither variant is advisory. `bare` proves an absent peer fails well, `full` proves a present one is reached, and a provider that is dead on arrival passes the first and fails the second.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:186:- The type check runs under `moduleResolution: "nodenext"` and again under `"bundler"`. NodeNext reads `exports.types` through the node condition; `bundler` is what a Next.js consumer uses and resolves differently, and a package can satisfy one and not the other.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:192:- Publishing a GitHub Release triggers one publish job. The release carries the tag and the notes; the version is the one already committed in `package.json`, so a release names a version a pull request agreed to rather than minting one at the point of no return. A draft release fires nothing, and one ticked as a pre-release is refused by the job condition before a runner starts.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:193:- `workflow_dispatch` exists for one case: re-running a publish that failed after the release already exists. The earlier objection to it - that a publish startable by hand takes whatever branch somebody picks in a dropdown - is answered by the guards rather than by removing the trigger. The version guard reads the ref name and refuses anything that is not a `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` tag, and the ancestry check refuses a commit that is not on `main`, so a branch chosen there fails closed. The one exception is the single-use bootstrap under § First publish, which is a separate workflow file, runs once, and is deleted in the same change that registers the trusted publisher.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:194:- Prereleases are refused rather than routed to a dist-tag, twice over: the job condition refuses a release ticked as a pre-release, and the version guard refuses the tag shape. Nothing here passes `--tag`, so a `v0.3.0-rc.1` would publish as `latest` and every plain `npm install` would resolve to it. Adding a dist-tag is a decision for whoever first needs one.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:195:- The guard reads the committed version with `node -p "require('./package.json').version"` rather than by parsing the file, so it reads it the way npm will.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:196:- The job authenticates with npm Trusted Publishing over OIDC. No `NPM_TOKEN` secret exists in the repository after bootstrap.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:197:- The version is staged rather than published outright. `npm stage publish` puts the tarball, its dist-tag and its provenance on the registry and leaves the version uninstallable until a maintainer approves it with 2FA, on the package page or through `npm stage approve`. The tag still triggers everything and CI still builds what ships; what changes is that the one irreversible step waits on a human holding a second factor, so a tag pushed by mistake costs an approval nobody gives rather than a version nobody can replace. It also puts proof-of-presence back into a flow that removed it - tokenless publishing has no human in it by design, which is the point of it and also its whole risk.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:198:- Staging needs npm >= 11.15.0, above the 11.5.1 trusted publishing needs, so the job installs `npm@^11.15.0` rather than `npm@^11.5.1`. It is pinned to the 11.x line rather than `latest` because this is the one job holding `id-token: write`.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:199:- `id-token: write` is scoped to the `publish` job rather than declared at workflow level, so the `verify` job - which runs the tests, the build and the packed-tarball smoke test - cannot mint an OIDC token at all. The issue specified a workflow-level pair; narrowing it costs nothing and removes a capability from every step that does not publish.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:200:- A guard fails the job when the tag and the committed `package.json` version disagree. It is a committed script rather than an inline step, so a developer can run it before pushing a tag they cannot un-push ([validated by: refuses a tag that disagrees with the committed version](../../scripts/check-version.test.ts#L36)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:201:- A matching tag is accepted ([validated by: accepts a tag that matches the version under a changelog heading naming it](../../scripts/check-version.test.ts#L32)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:202:- A prerelease tag is refused, because nothing here passes `--tag` and one would publish as `latest` ([validated by: refuses a prerelease tag, because nothing here passes a dist-tag](../../scripts/check-version.test.ts#L40)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:206:- A change to shipped source that updates the changelog is accepted ([validated by: accepts a change to shipped source that updates the changelog](../../scripts/check-changelog.test.ts#L49)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:207:- One that leaves the changelog alone is refused ([validated by: refuses a change to shipped source that leaves the changelog alone](../../scripts/check-changelog.test.ts#L56)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:208:- The report names the file that changed, so it says what to write about ([validated by: names the file that changed, so the report says what to write about](../../scripts/check-changelog.test.ts#L63)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:209:- A change to a test under `src/` is accepted, because it ships nothing a consumer observes ([validated by: accepts a change to a test under src, which ships nothing a consumer observes](../../scripts/check-changelog.test.ts#L70)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:210:- A base ref that does not resolve is skipped locally, where a fresh clone legitimately has no remote branch ([validated by: skips on a base ref that does not resolve, because a fresh clone has no remote branch](../../scripts/check-changelog.test.ts#L77)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:211:- The same case fails in CI. Skipping there is the gate passing without running, which is how a fetch that failed went unnoticed for a whole pull request ([validated by: fails on an unresolvable base ref in CI, rather than passing a gate that never ran](../../scripts/check-changelog.test.ts#L81)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:215:- A version the changelog never names is refused ([validated by: refuses a version the changelog never names](../../scripts/check-version.test.ts#L45)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:216:- The report names the heading that is missing ([validated by: says which heading is missing rather than only that something is wrong](../../scripts/check-version.test.ts#L49)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:217:- A heading matching the version only as a pattern does not satisfy it ([validated by: does not accept a heading that merely matches the version as a pattern](../../scripts/check-version.test.ts#L54)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:218:- A heading carrying no date is accepted, which Keep a Changelog allows ([validated by: accepts a heading carrying no date, which Keep a Changelog allows](../../scripts/check-version.test.ts#L58)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:219:- A guard fails the job when the tagged commit is not an ancestor of `origin/main`. A release is draftable against any branch or commit, so without it the protection on `main` is not the boundary the release rests on.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:220:- Whether the publish carries `--provenance` is decided by [ADR-007](../../adrs/ADR-007-repository-visibility.md), which makes the repository public, so it does. npm generates provenance only from a public source repository, and refuses it for any repository an unauthenticated client cannot read.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:221:- AGENTS.md gains a § Releasing section covering the five human steps: bump in the pull request, merge, draft the release on `main` tagged `vX.Y.Z`, publish it, approve the stage.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:229:- A hand-written `CHANGELOG.md` on Keep a Changelog 1.1.0 replaces the claim, `## [Unreleased]` first.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:230:- The argument is audience. A generator emits commit subjects, and this repository's read like `feat(build): require a test citation to land on the declaration` — accurate for a reviewer and meaningless to somebody installing the package. The two breaking changes in this release, the ESM conversion and the scope rename, each need a sentence telling a consumer what to do, which no commit subject contains.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:231:- AGENTS.md's line is replaced by the real rule rather than supplemented by a new contributing document.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:232:- A CI step fails a pull request that touches non-test files under `src/` without touching `CHANGELOG.md`. Test-only changes under `src/` do not trigger it, because tests live beside their source here and a consumer cannot observe them. The `no-changelog` label is the escape hatch, and applying it is a visible act rather than a silent omission.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:233:- The `## [Unreleased]` heading is renamed to the version being released as part of the release procedure T011 documents.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:239:- A single-use, manually triggered workflow publishes `0.2.0` once with a short-lived token.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:240:- It publishes rather than stages, which is the one place the bootstrap cannot follow the release path. `npm stage publish` answers `404 Package "@re-cinq/hal-engine" not found` against a name the registry has never seen: a stage is approved from the package's Versions tab, so there has to be a package. It was measured rather than assumed - the same workflow on the same commit moved from `403 Unauthorized` to that 404 when the token was corrected, which locates the refusal in the lookup rather than in the credential. So `0.2.0` is the only version that reaches the registry without an approval, and dispatching the workflow is that approval.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:241:- It runs from CI rather than a laptop, so the provenance attestation is produced from the GitHub OIDC token and `0.2.0` is not permanently unattested.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:242:- The token has to bypass two-factor authentication - `npm token create --bypass-2fa`, or the equivalent checkbox. A token without it answers `EOTP: This operation requires a one-time password`, and no CI job can supply one. Staging is what exists to defer proof-of-presence, and staging cannot create the package, so for this one publish the two mechanisms that would each solve it rule each other out. Presence is proven once, when the token is created.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:243:- The trusted publisher is then registered against the release workflow with staged publishing required, the token is revoked, the secret is deleted, and the single-use workflow is deleted.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:244:- A `v0.2.1` release then stages with no secret present in the repository, and is approved the same way. That is the evidence this step produces. `0.2.1` carries its own `## [0.2.1]` heading in the changelog, because `check-version.sh` requires one, and its entry says what it is: the first tokenless publish, with no change to the package.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:245:- The `v0.2.0` tag is pushed after the bootstrap, on the commit it published, and no release object is created for it. A tag with no release still renders at `/releases/tag/v0.2.0`, which is what the changelog links to, and the publish workflow no longer triggers on a tag push - so the link resolves and nothing runs. Creating a release instead would fire `publish.yml`, pass every gate against the tagged commit and fail at the staging step against a version that already exists: a permanently red run in the history, bought for a row on the releases page. This replaces the earlier plan to accept that red run, which predates the release-event trigger. The attestation on `0.2.0` names the bootstrap workflow, which no longer exists in the tree, and `npm audit signatures` still verifies it, because the attestation records the workflow at the commit it ran from.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:246:- Everything here happens once and cannot be redone: a published version cannot be replaced, and after 72 hours cannot be withdrawn. From `0.2.1` staging moves the point of no return from the release to the approval; for `0.2.0` it stays at the dispatch, which is why that workflow runs the full gate sequence rather than trusting the run CI did on the same commit. It therefore runs last, after the tarball smoke test passes and after the README that becomes the package's front page is correct.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:254:- The fallback becomes a middleware that answers `401`, for all three routes.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:255:- `createChatRoutes`, `createApp` and `HalEngineConfig` keep their signatures. This is a default change, not a type change.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:256:- A consumer terminating authentication at a gateway writes the pass-through themselves, one line, in their own repository.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:257:- `specs/hal-engine-chat-routes/spec.md` § When the guard does nothing currently describes the old default as behaviour and is corrected here.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:263:- The `'anonymous'` fallback is removed.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:264:- `AuthenticatedRequest` — `Request` plus optional `user` — is exported from the types layer and re-exported from the public surface, and `HttpAuthMiddleware` is restated in its terms, so the contract for what a middleware must produce is written down.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:265:- The four ad-hoc inline casts in `chats.ts` are replaced by that exported type.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:266:- A guard between the auth middleware and each handler answers `401` when `req.user` is absent or its `id` is `undefined`, `null` or `''`. The empty string is named explicitly because `id` is `string | number` and `0` is a legal id that a truthiness check would wrongly deny.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:272:- The export stays. It is public surface of a package about to be published, and the only worked example in the repository of lifting auth headers off an Express request into a `ChatSession`.
